# module Vendored source transports

This package owns materialising a dependency's locked Git tree inside the consumer as either a
submodule or a plain copy. CLI commands orchestrate metadata and adapter transactions; this package
owns Git/path safety, source identity, materialisation, drift, and residue-free removal.

# invariant Lock object identity is authoritative

Submodule state is the index gitlink at `vendor.path`, which must be mode 160000 and equal the
locked commit. Copy state is a Git tree id computed from index entries below `vendor.path`, with
their prefix removed and only `.git-a2a-vendored` excluded. The stamp is human-readable evidence,
not provenance. Worktree mode differences never substitute for index identity.

# invariant File remotes opt into Git's local protocol narrowly

Only a command operating on a `file://` source adds `-c protocol.file.allow=always`, and only to
that Git invocation. Global or repository Git configuration is never weakened.

# decision Copy archives preserve Git entry semantics

Materialise regular and executable blobs with their recorded modes and 120000 entries as symlinks.
Reject gitlinks because copy mode cannot materialise nested-submodule content. If the platform
cannot create a symlink, fail explicitly and roll back instead of writing its target as a file.

# decision Transport rollback owns every Git residue

A failed newly-added submodule is deinitialised, removed from the index and worktree, removed from
`.git/modules/<path>`, and removed from `.gitmodules`; the original `.gitmodules` bytes are restored.
Copy materialisation uses a temporary sibling and replaces the destination only after extraction
succeeds. Removal and destructive replacement refuse drift or dirty submodules unless the caller
passes the explicit force choice.

# test Transport regression suite

Use local bare repositories. Assert submodule gitlink identity, update, dirty refusal, recursive
file-protocol scoping, complete removal of `.gitmodules` and `.git/modules` residue, copy modes and
symlinks, copy tree drift, stamp exclusion, idempotence, unsafe/symlink path rejection, and rollback.
Run the lifecycle on Windows with `core.autocrlf=true`: lock hashes come from Git blobs, never
translated checkout bytes. After every mode change or removal, prune empty parents below
`.git/modules` so the directory is absent or empty.
